---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert AI visual director, social media designer and image-generation prompt specialist.

TASK:
Create a detailed AI image-generation prompt for a social media visual that is designed specifically for the selected platform, audience and communication goal.

The final visual must be visually strong, mobile-friendly, compositionally clear and suitable for real social media publishing.

SOCIAL PLATFORM:
[Facebook / Instagram / LinkedIn / TikTok / YouTube / Pinterest / X / other.]

CONTENT TYPE:
[Feed post / Reel cover / Story / carousel cover / promotional graphic / announcement / educational visual / quote graphic / other.]

PRIMARY GOAL:
[Awareness / engagement / traffic / promotion / education / lead generation / announcement / branding / other.]

TOPIC:
[What is the post about?]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who should notice and understand this post?]

MAIN MESSAGE:
[The single most important thing the viewer should understand.]

HEADLINE TEXT:
[Exact text to include, or "No visible text".]

SECONDARY TEXT:
[Optional.]

CTA TEXT:
[Optional.]

BRAND NAME:
[Optional.]

BRAND COLOURS:
[Hex codes or colour descriptions.]

LOGO:
[Describe logo placement if required.]

VISUAL STYLE:
[Professional / premium / editorial / SaaS / cinematic / lifestyle / minimal / bold / luxury / food-commercial / other.]

MAIN SUBJECT:
[Person / product / object / workspace / food / illustration / abstract visual / other.]

SUBJECT DESCRIPTION:
[Describe the subject accurately.]

SCENE:
[Describe the environment.]

COMPOSITION:
[Subject left / right / centred / split layout / asymmetric / other.]

ASPECT RATIO:
[1:1 / 4:5 / 9:16 / 16:9 / platform-specific.]

CAMERA / VIEW:
[Close-up / chest-up / wide / top-down / product hero / other.]

LIGHTING:
[Soft studio / natural daylight / dramatic / warm / clean commercial / other.]

BACKGROUND:
[Describe.]

TEXT POSITION:
[Top / left / right / lower third / centre / no text.]

NEGATIVE SPACE:
[Where clean space should remain.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

SOCIAL MEDIA VISUAL REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH THE PLATFORM
The design should reflect where the image will actually appear.

2. OPTIMIZE FOR MOBILE
Most important elements must remain readable at small feed size.

3. CREATE ONE CLEAR VISUAL PRIORITY
The viewer should immediately know where to look first.

4. DEFINE THE PRIMARY SUBJECT
Use one dominant visual subject unless the concept genuinely requires multiple elements.

5. DO NOT OVERCROWD
Avoid excessive:

Text
Icons
Objects
Badges
Decorative graphics
Background details

6. CREATE STRONG VISUAL HIERARCHY
Priority should usually be:

Primary subject
Headline
Supporting message
CTA
Brand

7. KEEP THE MAIN MESSAGE SIMPLE
A social visual should communicate one central idea quickly.

8. MATCH THE PLATFORM CONTEXT
Examples:

LinkedIn:
Professional, credible, clean, business-oriented.

Instagram:
Strong visual appeal, polished composition, lifestyle or brand quality.

Facebook:
Clear message, strong thumbnail readability, broad audience accessibility.

TikTok / Reels:
Vertical, high-impact, immediate focal point.

YouTube:
Thumbnail-focused, strong subject separation, expressive visual storytelling.

9. USE THE CORRECT ASPECT RATIO
Follow the user-specified ratio.

If not specified, recommend an appropriate ratio rather than silently choosing.

10. RESPECT SAFE AREAS
Keep essential text, faces, products and logos away from edges.

11. FOR VERTICAL VIDEO COVERS
Keep important information within the central mobile-safe area.

12. DESIGN FOR THUMBNAIL SIZE
The concept must remain understandable when viewed small.

13. USE LARGE, READABLE TEXT
If visible text is requested, make the headline short and high contrast.

14. DO NOT GENERATE LONG PARAGRAPHS INSIDE THE IMAGE
Complex copy should remain in the post caption rather than the artwork.

15. LIMIT TEXT
Prefer:

Headline
Short supporting phrase
CTA

16. PRESERVE EXACT TEXT
When exact text is supplied, reproduce it without rewriting.

17. DO NOT INVENT TEXT
Do not add random slogans, labels or marketing claims.

18. DO NOT ADD RANDOM LETTERING
Avoid meaningless AI-generated text in the environment.

19. CONTROL TYPOGRAPHIC HIERARCHY
Headline should dominate supporting copy.

20. USE PROFESSIONAL TYPOGRAPHY
Prefer clean, modern, readable font treatment appropriate to the brand.

21. KEEP FONT STYLES LIMITED
Avoid mixing many unrelated typefaces.

22. ENSURE CONTRAST
Text must remain readable against the background.

23. USE NEGATIVE SPACE
Reserve intentional clean space for headline or CTA.

24. DO NOT PLACE TEXT OVER BUSY AREAS
Avoid detailed hair, faces, product labels or clutter behind typography.

25. KEEP THE SUBJECT CLEAR
The subject should not compete with decorative effects.

26. APPLY RULE-OF-THIRDS WHEN USEFUL
Use balanced placement rather than default centring.

27. USE ASYMMETRY WHEN APPROPRIATE
For professional or editorial visuals, asymmetry can create stronger hierarchy.

28. USE CENTRED COMPOSITION WHEN APPROPRIATE
Centred layouts work well for:

Products
Announcements
Hero objects
Simple promotional graphics

29. CONTROL SUBJECT SCALE
The main subject should be large enough to remain recognizable on mobile.

30. AVOID TINY HUMAN FIGURES
Unless the environment itself is the story.

31. USE CLOSE-UP HUMAN EXPRESSIONS CAREFULLY
Expression should match the content goal.

32. KEEP PEOPLE NATURAL
Avoid:

Plastic skin
Unrealistic teeth
Exaggerated expressions
Over-beautification

33. USE REALISTIC HAND ANATOMY
If hands are visible:

Natural fingers
Correct grip
Believable interaction

34. PRESERVE CHARACTER IDENTITY
If a reference character is used, do not redesign the face.

35. PRESERVE PRODUCT IDENTITY
If a real product reference is supplied, preserve:

Shape
Colour
Packaging
Proportions
Key physical details

36. DO NOT INVENT PRODUCT FEATURES
Keep advertised visual features accurate.

37. USE STRONG SUBJECT-BACKGROUND SEPARATION
Achieve this through:

Lighting
Depth
Contrast
Focus
Negative space

38. USE DEPTH INTENTIONALLY
Background blur can help focus attention but should not feel artificial.

39. LIGHT THE MAIN SUBJECT FIRST
Lighting should support the intended focal point.

40. MATCH LIGHTING TO BRAND MOOD
Examples:

Clean white light = professional / technical.
Warm light = lifestyle / food / hospitality.
Dramatic contrast = entertainment / cinematic.
Soft daylight = approachable / natural.

41. AVOID EXCESSIVE GLOW
Do not use neon effects unless they support the requested style.

42. AVOID GENERIC FUTURISTIC AI VISUALS
Do not automatically use:

Blue holograms
Glowing brains
Robot heads
Floating circuits

unless specifically relevant.

43. USE MEANINGFUL VISUAL METAPHORS
Any metaphor should directly support the message.

44. BUSINESS VISUAL MODE
For business and professional content use:

Clean geometry
Structured composition
Subtle depth
Realistic workplace elements
Controlled colour palette

45. SAAS VISUAL MODE
Use:

Modern UI-inspired panels
Data elements
Clean diagrams
Soft gradients
Minimal graphic accents

without turning the image into a fake software screenshot unless requested.

46. EDUCATIONAL VISUAL MODE
Prioritize:

Clarity
Structure
Visual explanation
Readable labels

47. PROMOTIONAL VISUAL MODE
Prioritize:

Offer
Product
Benefit
CTA

Do not invent discounts or urgency.

48. ANNOUNCEMENT MODE
Make the announcement itself the clear visual priority.

49. PERSONAL BRAND MODE
Use the creator or professional as the primary visual anchor.

50. FOOD CONTENT MODE
Prioritize:

Appetite appeal
Texture
Freshness
Natural food styling
Realistic colour

51. PRODUCT CONTENT MODE
Prioritize:

Product shape
Material
Lighting
Surface texture
Commercial cleanliness

52. CREATOR CONTENT MODE
Use authentic, expressive, platform-native composition.

53. PROFESSIONAL LINKEDIN MODE
Prefer:

Premium business visual style
Clean layout
Strong headline
Credible imagery
Minimal clutter

54. INSTAGRAM MODE
Favor strong aesthetics while preserving message clarity.

55. FACEBOOK MODE
Use strong mobile readability and clear visual storytelling.

56. REEL COVER MODE
The image must work as a vertical thumbnail.

57. STORY MODE
Keep top and bottom interface zones clear where possible.

58. CAROUSEL COVER MODE
Make the first-slide promise obvious and visually strong.

59. THUMBNAIL MODE
Prioritize instant comprehension.

60. CONTROL COLOUR PALETTE
Use the requested brand colours intentionally.

61. USE ACCENT COLOURS SPARINGLY
Reserve them for:

CTA
Highlight
Result
Warning
Key data

62. DO NOT LET BRAND COLOURS OVERPOWER LEGIBILITY
Readability has priority.

63. PRESERVE BRAND CONSISTENCY
If brand identity is supplied, keep:

Colours
Logo style
Typography mood
Visual language

64. LOGO PLACEMENT
Keep logos visible but secondary to the content message.

65. DO NOT REDESIGN THE LOGO
If a logo reference is supplied, preserve it accurately.

66. DO NOT INVENT LOGOS
Do not create fake company marks.

67. CONTROL VISUAL DENSITY
Leave breathing room.

68. USE DECORATIVE ELEMENTS PURPOSEFULLY
Lines, gradients, shapes and icons should help hierarchy or meaning.

69. AVOID RANDOM DECORATION
Do not add unrelated icons or graphics merely to fill space.

70. USE ICONS CAREFULLY
Keep icon style consistent.

71. KEEP VISUAL STYLE COHERENT
Do not mix:

Photorealism
Cartoon
3D
Flat icons
Sketching

without an intentional design concept.

72. CONTROL REALISM
If photorealistic, maintain believable:

Lighting
Materials
Shadows
Reflections
Perspective

73. CONTROL SHADOWS
Keep shadows soft or strong according to the light source.

74. CONTROL REFLECTIONS
Use realistic reflections on:

Glass
Metal
Glossy packaging
Screens

75. KEEP PERSPECTIVE CONSISTENT
All objects should belong to the same scene geometry.

76. AVOID FLOATING OBJECTS
Unless intentionally designed as a graphic composition.

77. USE MOTION ONLY WHEN RELEVANT
Motion elements should support energy, not introduce visual chaos.

78. CONTROL BACKGROUND COMPLEXITY
Background should support, not compete with, the subject.

79. USE BLUR PURPOSEFULLY
Do not rely on excessive blur to hide poor composition.

80. KEEP TEXT AWAY FROM FACES
Unless intentionally designed and still readable.

81. KEEP CTA DISTINCT
CTA should be visible but not overpower the headline.

82. DO NOT CREATE FAKE BUTTONS
Unless the visual intentionally represents interface design.

83. DO NOT CREATE MISLEADING UI
Avoid interface elements that appear interactive when they are not.

84. DO NOT INVENT METRICS
If data is shown, use only supplied values.

85. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER RESULTS
Avoid fake:

Revenue
Growth
Ratings
Testimonials
Statistics

86. DO NOT CREATE FALSE SCARCITY
Avoid:

Only today
Last chance
Limited stock

unless supplied.

87. DO NOT CREATE FALSE URGENCY
Do not add countdowns or urgent claims unless requested.

88. DO NOT USE MISLEADING BEFORE/AFTER VISUALS
Unless supported by real source material.

89. KEEP ETHICAL REPRESENTATION
Do not create deceptive representations of products, results or people.

90. CREATE PLATFORM-SPECIFIC PROMPT LANGUAGE
Describe:

Aspect ratio
Subject size
Safe margins
Text hierarchy
Mobile readability

explicitly.

91. CREATE A COMPOSITION MAP
Before the final prompt define:

Top zone
Primary subject zone
Headline zone
Supporting-text zone
CTA zone
Brand zone

when relevant.

92. CREATE THE FINAL GENERATION PROMPT
Write one cohesive prompt that can be pasted into an image generator.

93. INCLUDE TEXT INSTRUCTIONS
If visible text is required, specify:

Exact wording
Position
Relative size
Hierarchy
Contrast

94. INCLUDE NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS
Use only relevant protections such as:

No extra text
No random logo
No watermark
No distorted hands
No duplicated people
No clutter
No illegible typography

95. KEEP NEGATIVE PROMPTS TARGETED
Do not create an unrelated keyword dump.

96. CREATE ALTERNATIVE COMPOSITION
Where useful, provide one alternate layout without changing the campaign idea.

97. PROVIDE CAPTION ALIGNMENT NOTES
Optionally explain what type of caption would complement the visual without writing unsupported claims.

98. CHECK MOBILE READABILITY
Imagine the artwork at small screen size.

99. CHECK BRAND FIT
Confirm that colour, typography, image style and composition feel coherent.

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- platform is defined
- aspect ratio is correct
- main subject is obvious
- headline hierarchy is strong
- visual remains readable on mobile
- safe margins are preserved
- unnecessary clutter is removed
- text is limited
- exact text is preserved
- random AI text is prevented
- brand colours are controlled
- logo is not redesigned
- composition has intentional negative space
- lighting supports the focal point
- background does not compete
- no unsupported claims are introduced
- no false urgency or scarcity is added
- people and hands are anatomically plausible
- product identity is preserved where relevant
- visual style is consistent
- CTA is clear where required

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. VISUAL STRATEGY
Briefly explain the visual concept.

2. COMPOSITION MAP
Describe where the main elements should appear.

3. FULL IMAGE GENERATION PROMPT
Provide one polished, model-ready generation prompt.

4. TEXT & TYPOGRAPHY INSTRUCTIONS
Include exact text, hierarchy, placement and readability guidance.

5. NEGATIVE / PROTECTION INSTRUCTIONS
Include only relevant constraints.

6. ALTERNATIVE COMPOSITION
Provide one optional alternative when useful.

7. FINAL PLATFORM CHECK
Confirm aspect ratio, safe area, mobile readability and main focal point.

IMPORTANT:
- Design for the actual social platform, not for a generic image.
- Keep one clear focal point.
- Optimize for mobile and thumbnail viewing.
- Preserve exact supplied text.
- Do not invent claims, offers, statistics, logos or urgency.
- Keep important elements inside safe margins.
- Use brand colours intentionally.
- Avoid visual clutter and generic AI imagery.
- Make the final prompt specific enough for a high-quality image-generation model to follow.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Define the platform first",
    description:
      "A LinkedIn graphic, Instagram post and Reel cover need different framing, hierarchy and safe-area decisions even when they communicate the same message.",
  },
  {
    title: "Choose one main message",
    description:
      "Decide what the viewer must understand first, then build the subject, headline and supporting elements around that single priority.",
  },
  {
    title: "Design for small screens",
    description:
      "Use large subjects, limited text, strong contrast and generous safe margins so the visual remains understandable when seen quickly in a mobile feed.",
  },
  {
    title: "Protect the brand",
    description:
      "Provide exact colours, wording and logo requirements so the generation prompt does not introduce random branding or inconsistent visual elements.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Platform: LinkedIn.",
    "Content type: Promotional graphic.",
    "Topic: Economic Order Quantity calculator.",
    "Main message: Ordering too much or too frequently increases inventory-related cost.",
    "Style: Premium professional SaaS.",
    "Brand colours: Blue, deep navy and white.",
    "Composition: Calculator result as the central business insight with supporting inventory visuals.",
    "Aspect ratio: 1:1.",
  ],

  output: [
    "VISUAL STRATEGY: Create a clean business-software visual explaining the balance between ordering cost and holding cost, with the recommended EOQ positioned as the visual solution.",
    "COMPOSITION: Use a strong central result area, supporting cost indicators around it, a concise headline in a clean safe zone and subtle warehouse/inventory context.",
    "MOBILE PRIORITY: Keep the headline short, the EOQ result large and all secondary detail visually subordinate.",
    "PROTECTION: No random logos, no invented financial figures, no tiny unreadable interface text and no generic futuristic AI imagery.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Start with the thumbnail",
    description:
      "If the main idea disappears when the visual becomes small, the composition is too complex for a social feed.",
  },
  {
    title: "Keep most explanation in the caption",
    description:
      "The artwork should attract attention and communicate the core point. Detailed explanations usually belong in the post text rather than inside the image.",
  },
  {
    title: "Use platform-specific layouts",
    description:
      "A vertical Reel cover needs central safe-area discipline, while a LinkedIn square can use more structured business-style spacing and information hierarchy.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Social Media Visual Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Create platform-specific AI image prompts for professional social media graphics, posts, Reel covers, promotional visuals and branded content."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="IMAGE GENERATION"
    title="Social Media Visual"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Create platform-ready AI visuals with stronger composition, mobile readability, safe zones, branding and message hierarchy."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Social media",
      "AI image design",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "LinkedIn graphics",
      "Facebook posts",
      "Instagram content",
      "Reel covers",
      "Promotional visuals",
      "Branded content",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Design for the feed, not just the image generator."
    introDescription="Turn your message into a platform-specific visual concept with intentional composition, safe margins, text hierarchy and mobile-first readability."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn a business tool into a LinkedIn-ready promotional visual."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Create social visuals people can understand at a glance."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need more control over the full image?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's AI Image Prompt Builder when you need broader control over subject, camera, lighting, environment and visual style without focusing specifically on social media layout."
    warningTitle="Good artwork can still fail in the feed"
    warningText="A visually impressive image may perform poorly if the subject is too small, the text is unreadable or important elements are cropped by the platform interface. Always review the final image at realistic mobile size before publishing."
  />
</BaseLayout>